    ingredients: "2 cups white rice flour (finely ground)\n1/2 cup cooked white rice (blended)\n1 tsp active dry yeast or 1/4 cup fresh toddy\n1 tbsp sugar\n1.5 cups fresh coconut milk (first and second extract)\n1/2 cup coconut water\n1/2 tsp fine sea salt\n4 fresh farm eggs (for egg hopper variations)\nFreshly ground black pepper and seeni sambol for serving",
    steps: "Step 1: Yeast & Coconut Sugar Activation - In a small glass bowl, combine active dry yeast, granulated sugar, and lukewarm fresh coconut water (or filtered water at 105°F / 40°C). Whisk gently and allow to bloom undisturbed in a warm, draft-free spot for 10-12 minutes until a thick, frothy, effervescent foam blankets the surface, signaling active fermentation.\nStep 2: Preparing the Rice Flour Base - In a large ceramic mixing bowl, whisk together fine white roasted rice flour and a pinch of sea salt. In a separate blender, puree cooled cooked white rice with 1/2 cup of thin coconut milk until completely smooth with zero graininess; this cooked rice paste is the traditional Sri Lankan secret for soft, spongy hopper centers.\nStep 3: Initial Batter Emulsion - Gradually pour the smooth cooked rice puree, bloomed yeast mixture, and remaining thin coconut milk into the rice flour bowl. Whisk vigorously in circular strokes for 3 minutes until an ultra-smooth, lump-free batter forms, resembling heavy cream in consistency.\nStep 4: The Crucial Warm Room Fermentation - Cover the mixing bowl tightly with a clean damp kitchen cloth. Place in a warm environment (ideally 80°F to 85°F / 27°C to 30°C) and let ferment undisturbed for 6 to 8 hours (or overnight). When ready, the batter must double in volume, release a sweet yeasty-coconut aroma, and be filled with thousands of tiny active champagne-like bubbles.\nStep 5: Final Conditioning with First-Press Coconut Cream - Gently stir down the fermented batter. Slowly whisk in thick first-press coconut cream (kara kiri) and 1/2 teaspoon fine sea salt. The finished hopper batter should be light and pourable, coating the back of a spoon with a silky, delicate film that drips in a steady stream.\nStep 6: Seasoning & Swirling the Appa Thachchiya - Heat a traditional non-stick or well-seasoned cast-iron hemispherical hopper pan (appa thachchiya) over medium-high heat. Dip a folded paper towel in coconut oil and lightly wipe the pan surface. Ladle 1/3 cup of batter directly into the center. Immediately grip both pan handles with kitchen mitts, lift the pan, and swirl it smoothly 360 degrees in a single, fluid circular motion so batter runs up the curved walls, leaving a thick puddle pooled in the base.\nStep 7: Egg Crown & Steam Dome Cooking - For plain hoppers, immediately cover with the domed lid. For classic egg hoppers, crack a whole fresh egg directly into the center puddle, season with freshly ground black pepper and sea salt, and cover. Reduce heat to medium-low. Cook undisturbed for 3 to 4 minutes: watch for steam escaping under the rim, indicating the thick center is cooked through while the outer lacy perimeter turns golden-brown, crispy, and detached from the rim.\nStep 8: Delicate Unmolding & Traditional Service - Lift the domed lid straight up to prevent condensation droplets from falling onto the hopper. Run a thin, flexible butter knife or palette spatula gently around the lacy rim to release it; tilt the pan and slide the hopper onto a warm plate. The result must feature paper-thin, crackling crispy edges surrounding a soft, pillowy, steamed coconut sponge center. Serve immediately with spicy seeni sambol, lunu miris, and creamy kiri hodi.",
