    rating_count: 45,
    ingredients: "3 cups fine white or red rice flour (roasted string hopper flour)\n1 tsp fine sea salt\n1.75 cups boiling hot water (or as needed)\n1 tbsp virgin coconut oil\nTraditional string hopper press (mould) and cane mats (wattiya)\nFor Serving: Kiri Hodi (coconut milk turmeric gravy), Pol Sambol, and Dhal curry",
    steps: "Step 1: Precision Flour Sifting & Salt Integration - Sift high-grade roasted red or white rice flour twice through a fine-mesh flour sieve into a wide, heatproof earthenware or stainless steel mixing bowl. Whisk in fine sea salt evenly throughout the dry flour. Double-sifting aerates the flour particles and eliminates compacted clumps, guaranteeing silky-smooth extrusion later.\nStep 2: Boiling Water Scalding (The Gelatinization Phase) - Bring freshly filtered water to a rolling boil (strictly 212°F / 100°C). Gradually pour the boiling water into the center of the flour in a steady stream while stirring briskly and continuously with the narrow handle of a sturdy wooden spoon. The boiling water partially gelatinizes the rice starches, developing elasticity without any wheat gluten.\nStep 3: Kneading into a Pliable Warm Dough - As soon as the dough is cool enough to handle safely (about 2 minutes), drizzle with pure coconut oil. Knead vigorously with the palms of your hands for 3 to 4 minutes against the bowl sides. The dough must feel velvety, completely non-sticky, supple, and warm—similar in texture to soft modeling clay. If dough feels stiff, knead in 1-2 tablespoons of hot water; if too sticky, dust with a whisper of roasted flour.\nStep 4: Moisture Preservation Rest - Form the warm dough into a compact cylinder and cover immediately with a clean, damp cotton kitchen towel. Rice dough dries out rapidly when exposed to air, which causes noodles to snap during pressing; keeping it covered retains essential moisture.\nStep 5: Extruding Delicate Lace Patties - Lightly brush traditional woven bamboo string hopper mats (wattiya) with a microscopic film of coconut oil to prevent sticking. Pack a portion of warm dough into the cylindrical barrel of a brass or stainless steel string hopper press fitted with the finest multi-hole disc. Hold the press 2 inches above a mat and squeeze the handles firmly, moving in smooth, concentric circular spirals from outer edge to center to create a lightweight, airy, circular lace noodle patty (approx. 4 inches in diameter).\nStep 6: Stacking & Steam Chamber Preparation - Fill the base of a tiered bamboo or aluminum string hopper steamer with 2 inches of water and bring to a vigorous, steaming boil over high heat. Stack the noodle-filled woven mats into the steamer rack, staggering them slightly so steam circulates freely through every layer without creating wet condensate pockets.\nStep 7: Gentle Timed Steam Cooking - Secure the steamer lid tightly. Steam over steady medium-high heat for exactly 8 to 10 minutes. Test doneness by touching a noodle lightly with a clean fingertip: the noodles must feel springy, firm, and resilient, with zero pasty stickiness.\nStep 8: Inversion Unmolding & Festive Plating - Turn off heat, remove the steamer lid carefully away from your face, and lift out the mats. Invert each mat upside-down onto a banana leaf-lined serving platter; give the back of the woven mat a gentle tap, and the delicate noodle nest will peel away effortlessly without breaking. Serve piping hot in stacks of 5 to 10 accompanied by rich coconut milk gravy (kiri hodi), fiery pol sambol, and spicy fish or chicken curry.",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Precision Flour Sifting & Salt Integration - Sift high-grade roasted red or white rice flour twice through a fine-mesh flour sieve into a wide, heatproof earthenware or stainless steel mixing bowl. Whisk in fine sea salt evenly throughout the dry flour. Double-sifting aerates the flour particles and eliminates compacted clumps, guaranteeing silky-smooth extrusion later.\nStep 2: Boiling Water Scalding (The Gelatinization Phase) - Bring freshly filtered water to a rolling boil (strictly 212°F / 100°C). Gradually pour the boiling water into the center of the flour in a steady stream while stirring briskly and continuously with the narrow handle of a sturdy wooden spoon. The boiling water partially gelatinizes the rice starches, developing elasticity without any wheat gluten.\nStep 3: Kneading into a Pliable Warm Dough - As soon as the dough is cool enough to handle safely (about 2 minutes), drizzle with pure coconut oil. Knead vigorously with the palms of your hands for 3 to 4 minutes against the bowl sides. The dough must feel velvety, completely non-sticky, supple, and warm—similar in texture to soft modeling clay. If dough feels stiff, knead in 1-2 tablespoons of hot water; if too sticky, dust with a whisper of roasted flour.\nStep 4: Moisture Preservation Rest - Form the warm dough into a compact cylinder and cover immediately with a clean, damp cotton kitchen towel. Rice dough dries out rapidly when exposed to air, which causes noodles to snap during pressing; keeping it covered retains essential moisture.\nStep 5: Extruding Delicate Lace Patties - Lightly brush traditional woven bamboo string hopper mats (wattiya) with a microscopic film of coconut oil to prevent sticking. Pack a portion of warm dough into the cylindrical barrel of a brass or stainless steel string hopper press fitted with the finest multi-hole disc. Hold the press 2 inches above a mat and squeeze the handles firmly, moving in smooth, concentric circular spirals from outer edge to center to create a lightweight, airy, circular lace noodle patty (approx. 4 inches in diameter).\nStep 6: Stacking & Steam Chamber Preparation - Fill the base of a tiered bamboo or aluminum string hopper steamer with 2 inches of water and bring to a vigorous, steaming boil over high heat. Stack the noodle-filled woven mats into the steamer rack, staggering them slightly so steam circulates freely through every layer without creating wet condensate pockets.\nStep 7: Gentle Timed Steam Cooking - Secure the steamer lid tightly. Steam over steady medium-high heat for exactly 8 to 10 minutes. Test doneness by touching a noodle lightly with a clean fingertip: the noodles must feel springy, firm, and resilient, with zero pasty stickiness.\nStep 8: Inversion Unmolding & Festive Plating - Turn off heat, remove the steamer lid carefully away from your face, and lift out the mats. Invert each mat upside-down onto a banana leaf-lined serving platter; give the back of the woven mat a gentle tap, and the delicate noodle nest will peel away effortlessly without breaking. Serve piping hot in stacks of 5 to 10 accompanied by rich coconut milk gravy (kiri hodi), fiery pol sambol, and spicy fish or chicken curry."
