    avg_rating: 4.8,
    rating_count: 34,
    ingredients: "800g bone-in lamb shoulder (cut into 2-inch chunks)\n3 tbsp mustard oil or pure ghee\n2 black cardamoms & 4 green cardamoms\n4 whole cloves & 1 cinnamon stick\n1 cup plain Greek yogurt (whisked)\n2 tbsp Kashmiri red chili powder (for deep crimson hue)\n1.5 tsp ground fennel seeds (saunf)\n1 tsp ground dry ginger (soonth)\n1/2 tsp asafoetida (hing)\n1 tsp sea salt & saffron basmati rice for serving",
    steps: "Step 1: Heating Mustard Oil & Tempering Spices - Heat authentic pure mustard oil in a heavy 5-quart Dutch oven or handi over high heat until it reaches its smoking point (this burns off pungent allyl isothiocyanate fumes). Turn off heat and let cool for 2 minutes. Return to medium heat and add black cardamom pods, green cardamoms, whole cloves, cinnamon stick, bay leaves, and asafoetida (hing). Sizzle for 30 seconds until whole spices crackle and perfume the oil.\nStep 2: Searing Bone-In Lamb Chunks - Add dry bone-in lamb shoulder or shank chunks to the hot spiced oil in a single layer. Sear over medium-high heat for 8 to 10 minutes, turning occasionally, until meat develops an even, deep golden-brown exterior and rendered meat juices run clear.\nStep 3: Preparing the Kashmiri Chili & Saffron Slurry - In a small bowl, whisk authentic Kashmiri red chili powder (prized for vibrant ruby color and mild warmth) and ground ginger (sonth) with 1/4 cup warm water to form a smooth paste. In another cup, steep saffron threads in 2 tablespoons warm milk for 10 minutes.\nStep 4: Incorporating the Spice Paste - Pour the chili paste into the sizzling meat. Sauté over medium heat for 2 minutes, stirring constantly so spices bloom in the hot oil without scorching, coating each piece of lamb in brilliant red lacquer.\nStep 5: Whisking & Tempering Yogurt - In a bowl, vigorously whisk full-fat plain yogurt with ground fennel powder (saunf) and ground coriander until completely smooth with zero lumps. Reduce Dutch oven heat to low. Add yogurt one tablespoon at a time while stirring briskly in a single direction to prevent curdling. Sauté for 5 minutes until oil beads separate from the spiced yogurt sauce.\nStep 6: Dum Cooking & Low-Flame Braise - Pour in 1.5 cups of warm mutton stock or water and season with sea salt. Bring to a simmer. Cover Dutch oven with a heavy, tight-fitting lid (optionally seal rim with dough for traditional dum cooking). Reduce heat to the lowest setting and braise undisturbed for 50 to 60 minutes until lamb is meltingly fork-tender.\nStep 7: Saffron Infusion & Resting - Uncover pot and skim excess oil if desired (traditional Rogan Josh retains the 'Rogan' or glistening red oil layer). Stir in steeped saffron milk and a pinch of freshly ground green cardamom powder. Simmer uncovered for 3 minutes until gravy achieves a rich, velvety consistency.\nStep 8: Plating with Saffron Basmati Rice - Transfer fragrant Rogan Josh to a warm serving handi. Garnish with fresh coriander leaves. Serve alongside long-grain saffron basmati rice and warm garlic butter naan.",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Heating Mustard Oil & Tempering Spices - Heat authentic pure mustard oil in a heavy 5-quart Dutch oven or handi over high heat until it reaches its smoking point (this burns off pungent allyl isothiocyanate fumes). Turn off heat and let cool for 2 minutes. Return to medium heat and add black cardamom pods, green cardamoms, whole cloves, cinnamon stick, bay leaves, and asafoetida (hing). Sizzle for 30 seconds until whole spices crackle and perfume the oil.\nStep 2: Searing Bone-In Lamb Chunks - Add dry bone-in lamb shoulder or shank chunks to the hot spiced oil in a single layer. Sear over medium-high heat for 8 to 10 minutes, turning occasionally, until meat develops an even, deep golden-brown exterior and rendered meat juices run clear.\nStep 3: Preparing the Kashmiri Chili & Saffron Slurry - In a small bowl, whisk authentic Kashmiri red chili powder (prized for vibrant ruby color and mild warmth) and ground ginger (sonth) with 1/4 cup warm water to form a smooth paste. In another cup, steep saffron threads in 2 tablespoons warm milk for 10 minutes.\nStep 4: Incorporating the Spice Paste - Pour the chili paste into the sizzling meat. Sauté over medium heat for 2 minutes, stirring constantly so spices bloom in the hot oil without scorching, coating each piece of lamb in brilliant red lacquer.\nStep 5: Whisking & Tempering Yogurt - In a bowl, vigorously whisk full-fat plain yogurt with ground fennel powder (saunf) and ground coriander until completely smooth with zero lumps. Reduce Dutch oven heat to low. Add yogurt one tablespoon at a time while stirring briskly in a single direction to prevent curdling. Sauté for 5 minutes until oil beads separate from the spiced yogurt sauce.\nStep 6: Dum Cooking & Low-Flame Braise - Pour in 1.5 cups of warm mutton stock or water and season with sea salt. Bring to a simmer. Cover Dutch oven with a heavy, tight-fitting lid (optionally seal rim with dough for traditional dum cooking). Reduce heat to the lowest setting and braise undisturbed for 50 to 60 minutes until lamb is meltingly fork-tender.\nStep 7: Saffron Infusion & Resting - Uncover pot and skim excess oil if desired (traditional Rogan Josh retains the 'Rogan' or glistening red oil layer). Stir in steeped saffron milk and a pinch of freshly ground green cardamom powder. Simmer uncovered for 3 minutes until gravy achieves a rich, velvety consistency.\nStep 8: Plating with Saffron Basmati Rice - Transfer fragrant Rogan Josh to a warm serving handi. Garnish with fresh coriander leaves. Serve alongside long-grain saffron basmati rice and warm garlic butter naan."
