    ingredients: "400g dark Sri Lankan kithul palm jaggery (finely shaved or grated)\n6 large farm eggs (room temperature)\n1.5 cups thick coconut cream (freshly squeezed first press)\n1/2 tsp freshly grated nutmeg\n1/2 tsp freshly ground green cardamom powder\n1/4 tsp ground cloves\n1 tbsp pure vanilla extract or rose water\n1/2 cup roasted unsalted cashew nuts (halved for topping)",
    steps: "Step 1: Melting & Straining Kithul Jaggery - In a heavy saucepan over low heat, combine finely shaved dark Sri Lankan Kithul palm jaggery with 3 tablespoons of filtered water. Stir continuously with a wooden spoon for 4 to 5 minutes until jaggery melts into a dark, glossy, fragrant amber syrup. Pour hot syrup through a fine muslin cloth or stainless steel sieve into a bowl to remove palm fibers and sediment; cool completely to room temperature.\nStep 2: Preparing Spices in Stone Mortar - In a granite pestle and mortar, freshly pound whole green cardamom seeds, whole cloves, and a grating of whole nutmeg into an ultra-fine, fragrant aromatic powder. Freshly pounded spices provide the authentic, warm Sri Lankan floral aroma.\nStep 3: Gentle Egg Whisking (Zero Aeration) - Crack farm-fresh eggs into a wide mixing bowl. Whisk gently with a hand balloon whisk just until yolks and whites are combined into a uniform liquid. Crucial culinary rule: avoid vigorous whipping or beating; incorporating air bubbles creates an undesirable porous honeycomb texture, whereas authentic Watalappan requires a dense, velvety, glass-smooth custard.\nStep 4: Incorporating Thick Coconut Cream & Jaggery - Slowly pour cooled Kithul jaggery syrup and thick, first-press coconut cream into the whisked eggs in a steady stream. Whisk gently until harmoniously emulsified into a dark mocha-colored custard. Whisk in the freshly ground spices, pure vanilla extract, and a light pinch of sea salt to balance sweetness.\nStep 5: Double-Straining for Silken Texture - Pour the custard mixture through a fine-mesh sieve into a clean pitcher. Repeat the straining process a second time into a buttered heatproof ceramic baking dish or traditional metal pudding mould. Straining removes chalazae and foam, guaranteeing an unblemished, silky-smooth set.\nStep 6: Moisture-Proof Foil Sealing - Cover the pudding mould tightly with two layers of heavy-duty aluminum foil, crimping the edges securely around the rim. This prevents condensed steam droplets from dripping onto the custard surface, which causes pitting.\nStep 7: Gentle Steaming / Bain-Marie Cooking - Place the mould into a tiered steamer over simmering water (or into a water bath in an oven preheated to 325°F / 165°C). Cook over steady medium-low steam for 40 to 45 minutes. Test doneness by inserting a thin wooden skewer into the center: it must emerge clean and warm, with the custard showing a gentle jiggle.\nStep 8: Cashew Decoration & Chilling - Remove mould from steamer and unwrap foil carefully. Press golden-roasted cashew halves decoratively across the warm custard surface in concentric circles. Cool to room temperature, then refrigerate for at least 4 hours (preferably overnight). Slice into dark, rich, velvety squares and serve chilled.",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Melting & Straining Kithul Jaggery - In a heavy saucepan over low heat, combine finely shaved dark Sri Lankan Kithul palm jaggery with 3 tablespoons of filtered water. Stir continuously with a wooden spoon for 4 to 5 minutes until jaggery melts into a dark, glossy, fragrant amber syrup. Pour hot syrup through a fine muslin cloth or stainless steel sieve into a bowl to remove palm fibers and sediment; cool completely to room temperature.\nStep 2: Preparing Spices in Stone Mortar - In a granite pestle and mortar, freshly pound whole green cardamom seeds, whole cloves, and a grating of whole nutmeg into an ultra-fine, fragrant aromatic powder. Freshly pounded spices provide the authentic, warm Sri Lankan floral aroma.\nStep 3: Gentle Egg Whisking (Zero Aeration) - Crack farm-fresh eggs into a wide mixing bowl. Whisk gently with a hand balloon whisk just until yolks and whites are combined into a uniform liquid. Crucial culinary rule: avoid vigorous whipping or beating; incorporating air bubbles creates an undesirable porous honeycomb texture, whereas authentic Watalappan requires a dense, velvety, glass-smooth custard.\nStep 4: Incorporating Thick Coconut Cream & Jaggery - Slowly pour cooled Kithul jaggery syrup and thick, first-press coconut cream into the whisked eggs in a steady stream. Whisk gently until harmoniously emulsified into a dark mocha-colored custard. Whisk in the freshly ground spices, pure vanilla extract, and a light pinch of sea salt to balance sweetness.\nStep 5: Double-Straining for Silken Texture - Pour the custard mixture through a fine-mesh sieve into a clean pitcher. Repeat the straining process a second time into a buttered heatproof ceramic baking dish or traditional metal pudding mould. Straining removes chalazae and foam, guaranteeing an unblemished, silky-smooth set.\nStep 6: Moisture-Proof Foil Sealing - Cover the pudding mould tightly with two layers of heavy-duty aluminum foil, crimping the edges securely around the rim. This prevents condensed steam droplets from dripping onto the custard surface, which causes pitting.\nStep 7: Gentle Steaming / Bain-Marie Cooking - Place the mould into a tiered steamer over simmering water (or into a water bath in an oven preheated to 325°F / 165°C). Cook over steady medium-low steam for 40 to 45 minutes. Test doneness by inserting a thin wooden skewer into the center: it must emerge clean and warm, with the custard showing a gentle jiggle.\nStep 8: Cashew Decoration & Chilling - Remove mould from steamer and unwrap foil carefully. Press golden-roasted cashew halves decoratively across the warm custard surface in concentric circles. Cool to room temperature, then refrigerate for at least 4 hours (preferably overnight). Slice into dark, rich, velvety squares and serve chilled."
