    ingredients: "1 sheet artisanal strudel dough or phyllo pastry sheets\n1 kg tart cooking apples (Granny Smith, peeled, cored, thinly sliced)\n1/2 cup sultana raisins (soaked in Austrian rum)\n1/2 cup fine breadcrumbs toasted in 2 tbsp melted butter\n1/2 cup granulated sugar & 1 tsp ground Ceylon cinnamon\n4 tbsp unsalted melted butter for brushing\nPowdered sugar and warm vanilla custard sauce for serving",
    steps: "Step 1: Macerating Apples & Rum Raisins - In a bowl, soak golden raisins in dark Austrian rum for 20 minutes until plump. Peel, core, and slice firm-tart apples (Elstar, Braeburn, or Granny Smith) into thin 1/8-inch slices. Toss sliced apples with rum raisins, brown sugar, ground Ceylon cinnamon, lemon juice, and finely grated lemon zest; let sit for 15 minutes.\nStep 2: Toasting Buttery Breadcrumbs (Semmelbrösel) - In a skillet over medium heat, melt 3 tablespoons of unsalted European butter. Add fine breadcrumbs and stir continuously for 3 to 4 minutes until toasted to a nutty, aromatic golden-brown. Remove from heat and cool; these breadcrumbs absorb excess apple moisture during baking, preventing soggy strudel pastry.\nStep 3: Paper-Thin Strudel Dough Stretching - On a clean linen kitchen tablecloth dusted lightly with flour, roll strudel dough into a rectangle. Slip the floured backs of your hands beneath the dough and gently, patiently stretch the dough outward from the center until it is translucent and paper-thin (you should be able to read newspaper print through it).\nStep 4: Buttering & Breadcrumb Layering - Brush the stretched dough generously with melted butter. Scatter the toasted breadcrumbs evenly across the lower third of the dough sheet, leaving a 2-inch border along the sides.\nStep 5: Distributing Apple Filling & Folding - Spread macerated apple filling evenly over the breadcrumb bed. Fold the side margins of the dough inward over the filling to seal the edges.\nStep 6: Rolling the Strudel via Cloth Lift - Grasp the edge of the linen cloth beneath the filling. Lift the cloth slowly and steadily, allowing the strudel to roll up onto itself into a tight, neat spiral cylinder. Roll directly onto a parchment-lined baking sheet, seam-side down.\nStep 7: Butter Basting & Crispy Baking - Brush exterior of strudel generously with melted butter. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 35 to 40 minutes, basting with melted butter twice during baking, until pastry is flaky, crisp, and deep golden-brown.\nStep 8: Powdered Sugar Dusting & Bourbon Vanilla Sauce - Let cool for 15 minutes on a wire rack. Dust heavily with powdered sugar through a fine sieve. Slice diagonally into thick portions with a serrated bread knife and serve warm alongside rich, homemade bourbon vanilla bean custard sauce (vanillesauce).",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Macerating Apples & Rum Raisins - In a bowl, soak golden raisins in dark Austrian rum for 20 minutes until plump. Peel, core, and slice firm-tart apples (Elstar, Braeburn, or Granny Smith) into thin 1/8-inch slices. Toss sliced apples with rum raisins, brown sugar, ground Ceylon cinnamon, lemon juice, and finely grated lemon zest; let sit for 15 minutes.\nStep 2: Toasting Buttery Breadcrumbs (Semmelbrösel) - In a skillet over medium heat, melt 3 tablespoons of unsalted European butter. Add fine breadcrumbs and stir continuously for 3 to 4 minutes until toasted to a nutty, aromatic golden-brown. Remove from heat and cool; these breadcrumbs absorb excess apple moisture during baking, preventing soggy strudel pastry.\nStep 3: Paper-Thin Strudel Dough Stretching - On a clean linen kitchen tablecloth dusted lightly with flour, roll strudel dough into a rectangle. Slip the floured backs of your hands beneath the dough and gently, patiently stretch the dough outward from the center until it is translucent and paper-thin (you should be able to read newspaper print through it).\nStep 4: Buttering & Breadcrumb Layering - Brush the stretched dough generously with melted butter. Scatter the toasted breadcrumbs evenly across the lower third of the dough sheet, leaving a 2-inch border along the sides.\nStep 5: Distributing Apple Filling & Folding - Spread macerated apple filling evenly over the breadcrumb bed. Fold the side margins of the dough inward over the filling to seal the edges.\nStep 6: Rolling the Strudel via Cloth Lift - Grasp the edge of the linen cloth beneath the filling. Lift the cloth slowly and steadily, allowing the strudel to roll up onto itself into a tight, neat spiral cylinder. Roll directly onto a parchment-lined baking sheet, seam-side down.\nStep 7: Butter Basting & Crispy Baking - Brush exterior of strudel generously with melted butter. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 35 to 40 minutes, basting with melted butter twice during baking, until pastry is flaky, crisp, and deep golden-brown.\nStep 8: Powdered Sugar Dusting & Bourbon Vanilla Sauce - Let cool for 15 minutes on a wire rack. Dust heavily with powdered sugar through a fine sieve. Slice diagonally into thick portions with a serrated bread knife and serve warm alongside rich, homemade bourbon vanilla bean custard sauce (vanillesauce)."
