    rating_count: 66,
    ingredients: "500g all-butter puff pastry (rolled into a tight log and sliced into 12 rounds)\nFor Portuguese Custard Filling: 1 cup whole milk, 1 cup heavy cream, 3 tbsp all-purpose flour, 1 cup sugar, 1/2 cup water, 1 cinnamon stick, 1 strip lemon peel (yellow zest only), 6 large egg yolks (room temperature)\nGround Ceylon cinnamon and powdered sugar for dusting",
    steps: "Step 1: Spiral Lamination of Puff Pastry - Roll high-butter puff pastry dough into a 1/8-inch thick rectangle. Brush surface with softened European butter. Roll dough tightly into a compact spiral cylinder (like a tight jelly roll). Wrap in cling film and chill in refrigerator for 30 minutes to firm the butter layers.\nStep 2: Cutting & Pressing Pastry Discs - Trim ends and cut chilled dough log crosswise into 12 equal 1/2-inch rounds. Place each round spiral-face-up into the wells of a traditional Portuguese tin or heavy muffin tin. Using your thumbs moistened with cold water, press the center of the dough down against the base and work it smoothly up the flared sides, creating a thin pastry cup with a raised rim over the top.\nStep 3: Brewing Cinnamon-Citrus Sugar Syrup - In a saucepan, combine granulated sugar, filtered water, a Ceylon cinnamon stick, and a wide strip of lemon peel (avoid white pith). Boil over medium-high heat without stirring until temperature reaches 220°F (105°C) (approx. 3 minutes). Discard cinnamon and lemon peel; let syrup cool slightly.\nStep 4: Milk & Flour Thickening - In a separate saucepan, whisk whole milk with all-purpose flour and fine sea salt until smooth. Cook over medium heat, whisking constantly for 3 to 4 minutes until thick, smooth, and bubbling.\nStep 5: Emulsifying the Custard - Gradually pour warm sugar syrup into the hot milk paste while whisking continuously. In a separate bowl, whisk 6 egg yolks; temper yolks with a ladle of the warm mixture, then whisk tempered yolks into the custard until glossy, smooth, and liquid. Strain through a fine-mesh sieve.\nStep 6: Filling Pastry Shells - Pour cooled custard into the prepared pastry cups, filling each exactly 3/4 full to allow for bubbling during baking.\nStep 7: Blistering in Ultra-High Heat - Preheat oven to maximum temperature—strictly 500°F to 550°F (260°C to 290°C), preferably with a baking stone on the top rack. Bake tarts on the top rack for 10 to 14 minutes until pastry edges puff into crisp, flaky layers and the custard surfaces caramelize with deep, dark blistered patches (brulee spots).\nStep 8: Cooling & Lisbon-Style Service - Remove from oven and let tarts cool in the pan for 10 minutes until custard settles from puffy to flat. Dust warm Pastéis de Nata with ground cinnamon and powdered sugar, and serve warm with strong espresso."
