    avg_rating: 4.9,
    rating_count: 42,
    ingredients: "1.5 cups red split lentils (Mysore dhal, thoroughly washed)\n1 medium red shallot or onion (finely diced)\n2 fresh green bird's eye chilies (slit lengthwise)\n3 cloves garlic (crushed)\n1/2 tsp ground turmeric\n1/2 tsp fenugreek seeds (ulu hal)\n1 sprig fresh green curry leaves\n1 piece pandan leaf (2 inches)\n1.5 cups thin coconut milk or water\n1 cup thick coconut cream (first extract)\n1 tsp fine sea salt\nFor the Tempering (Theldala): 2 tbsp coconut oil, 1 tsp black mustard seeds, 2 dried red chilies (halved), 1 small sliced shallot, 1 sprig curry leaves",
    steps: "Step 1: Lentil Washing & Starch Rinsing - Measure red split lentils (masoor dhal) into a fine-mesh colander. Rinse under cold running water for 2 minutes, agitating with your fingers, until the runoff water transitions from cloudy to crystal clear. Drain thoroughly; washing eliminates excess surface starch and guarantees a clean, creamy texture without murkiness.\nStep 2: Clay Pot Infusion of Aromatics - Transfer rinsed lentils into a traditional Sri Lankan earthenware clay pot (chatti) or heavy saucepan. Add finely sliced shallots, slit green bird's eye chilies, minced garlic cloves, whole fenugreek seeds (methi), turmeric powder, fresh curry leaves, and a 2-inch bruised pandan leaf (rampe).\nStep 3: Thin Coconut Milk Simmer - Pour in thin coconut milk (diya kiri / second press) until lentils are submerged under 1 inch of liquid. Season with a light pinch of sea salt. Place pot over medium heat and bring to a gentle boil.\nStep 4: Tender Cooking Phase - Reduce heat to low, partially cover with clay lid, and simmer gently for 12 to 15 minutes. Stir occasionally with a wooden spoon. Cook until the lentils soften, turn from coral to pale golden-yellow, and absorb most of the thin milk into a creamy porridge-like consistency.\nStep 5: Thick Coconut Cream Enrichment - Pour in thick coconut cream (miti kiri / first press). Stir gently to integrate. Simmer uncovered over low heat for exactly 4 to 5 minutes, allowing the rich coconut cream to meld with the lentils into a luxurious, glossy golden gravy. Season with sea salt to taste, then remove pot from heat.\nStep 6: Preparing the Smoking Theldala Temper - Heat 2 tablespoons of pure coconut oil in a small tempering pan (thachchiya) over medium-high heat until hot. Add black mustard seeds and cover with a splatter guard for 10 seconds as they pop energetically.\nStep 7: Sizzle & Aromatic Bloom - Immediately add sliced shallots, whole dried red chilies (halved), and fresh curry leaves to the popping mustard seeds. Fry briskly for 60 to 75 seconds until shallots turn crispy golden-brown and curry leaves become dark, translucent, and crackling.\nStep 8: Pouring the Sizzle & Final Swirl - Pour the smoking, aromatic tempered oil immediately over the bubbling yellow lentil curry; it will hiss and sizzle vigorously as essential oils infuse the dish. Stir once or twice with a wooden spoon to marble the red spices into the golden dhal. Serve piping hot with red samba rice, hoppers, or pol roti.",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Lentil Washing & Starch Rinsing - Measure red split lentils (masoor dhal) into a fine-mesh colander. Rinse under cold running water for 2 minutes, agitating with your fingers, until the runoff water transitions from cloudy to crystal clear. Drain thoroughly; washing eliminates excess surface starch and guarantees a clean, creamy texture without murkiness.\nStep 2: Clay Pot Infusion of Aromatics - Transfer rinsed lentils into a traditional Sri Lankan earthenware clay pot (chatti) or heavy saucepan. Add finely sliced shallots, slit green bird's eye chilies, minced garlic cloves, whole fenugreek seeds (methi), turmeric powder, fresh curry leaves, and a 2-inch bruised pandan leaf (rampe).\nStep 3: Thin Coconut Milk Simmer - Pour in thin coconut milk (diya kiri / second press) until lentils are submerged under 1 inch of liquid. Season with a light pinch of sea salt. Place pot over medium heat and bring to a gentle boil.\nStep 4: Tender Cooking Phase - Reduce heat to low, partially cover with clay lid, and simmer gently for 12 to 15 minutes. Stir occasionally with a wooden spoon. Cook until the lentils soften, turn from coral to pale golden-yellow, and absorb most of the thin milk into a creamy porridge-like consistency.\nStep 5: Thick Coconut Cream Enrichment - Pour in thick coconut cream (miti kiri / first press). Stir gently to integrate. Simmer uncovered over low heat for exactly 4 to 5 minutes, allowing the rich coconut cream to meld with the lentils into a luxurious, glossy golden gravy. Season with sea salt to taste, then remove pot from heat.\nStep 6: Preparing the Smoking Theldala Temper - Heat 2 tablespoons of pure coconut oil in a small tempering pan (thachchiya) over medium-high heat until hot. Add black mustard seeds and cover with a splatter guard for 10 seconds as they pop energetically.\nStep 7: Sizzle & Aromatic Bloom - Immediately add sliced shallots, whole dried red chilies (halved), and fresh curry leaves to the popping mustard seeds. Fry briskly for 60 to 75 seconds until shallots turn crispy golden-brown and curry leaves become dark, translucent, and crackling.\nStep 8: Pouring the Sizzle & Final Swirl - Pour the smoking, aromatic tempered oil immediately over the bubbling yellow lentil curry; it will hiss and sizzle vigorously as essential oils infuse the dish. Stir once or twice with a wooden spoon to marble the red spices into the golden dhal. Serve piping hot with red samba rice, hoppers, or pol roti."
