    difficulty: "Easy",
    author_name: "Barista Han Lin",
    image_url: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Bubble_tea_by_Johnny.jpg/960px-Bubble_tea_by_Johnny.jpg",
    avg_rating: 4.9,
    rating_count: 40,
    ingredients: "1/2 cup black tapioca boba pearls\n1/2 cup dark brown sugar (Okinawa or Muscovado)\n1/2 cup water\n2 cups fresh whole milk or oat milk\n1 cup brewed strong black Assam tea (chilled)\nCrushed ice for serving",
    steps: "Step 1: Boiling Water to Rolling Boil - In a heavy 3-quart saucepan, bring 6 cups of filtered water to a rolling boil over high heat (a generous water-to-boba ratio of 6:1 prevents tapioca pearls from sticking together during cooking).\nStep 2: Cooking Tapioca Boba Pearls - Slowly scatter black tapioca pearls into the boiling water while stirring gently with a wooden spoon so they do not sink and stick. Once all pearls float to the surface (approx. 60 seconds), reduce heat to medium. Simmer uncovered for exactly 15 minutes, stirring occasionally.\nStep 3: The Crucial Lid-Covered Steam Rest - Remove saucepan from heat and cover tightly with the lid. Let tapioca pearls rest undisturbed in hot water for 15 minutes; this resting phase allows the heat to penetrate to the very core of each pearl, producing the signature chewy, springy 'QQ' texture with no chalky center.\nStep 4: Draining & Cold Rinse - Drain pearls through a fine-mesh colander. Rinse quickly under cold tap water for 10 seconds to stop cooking and wash away surface starch; drain thoroughly.\nStep 5: Simmering Thick Brown Sugar Syrup - In a small saucepan over medium heat, combine authentic dark muscovado or Taiwanese black sugar with 1/2 cup water. Bring to a simmer, stirring until sugar dissolves. Add drained boba pearls and simmer over medium-low heat for 6 to 8 minutes, stirring frequently, until syrup reduces to a thick, glossy, molasses-like consistency that clings to the pearls.\nStep 6: Creating Tiger Stripe Glass Marbling - Spoon 3 tablespoons of warm boba pearls and thick brown sugar syrup into the bottom of chilled tall glasses. Hold each glass horizontally and rotate it slowly, tilting to let the viscous black sugar syrup trail down the interior glass walls, creating striking tiger stripe marbling.\nStep 7: Ice Loading & Chilled Milk Pour - Pack glasses with crushed or cubed ice. Pour chilled fresh whole milk (or rich oat milk) gently over the ice until glass is nearly full, followed by a splash of concentrated brewed black Assam tea.\nStep 8: Service with Oversized Boba Straw - Insert an extra-wide boba straw to the bottom. Serve immediately while boba pearls are warm and chewy at the bottom and milk is ice-cold on top.",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Boiling Water to Rolling Boil - In a heavy 3-quart saucepan, bring 6 cups of filtered water to a rolling boil over high heat (a generous water-to-boba ratio of 6:1 prevents tapioca pearls from sticking together during cooking).\nStep 2: Cooking Tapioca Boba Pearls - Slowly scatter black tapioca pearls into the boiling water while stirring gently with a wooden spoon so they do not sink and stick. Once all pearls float to the surface (approx. 60 seconds), reduce heat to medium. Simmer uncovered for exactly 15 minutes, stirring occasionally.\nStep 3: The Crucial Lid-Covered Steam Rest - Remove saucepan from heat and cover tightly with the lid. Let tapioca pearls rest undisturbed in hot water for 15 minutes; this resting phase allows the heat to penetrate to the very core of each pearl, producing the signature chewy, springy 'QQ' texture with no chalky center.\nStep 4: Draining & Cold Rinse - Drain pearls through a fine-mesh colander. Rinse quickly under cold tap water for 10 seconds to stop cooking and wash away surface starch; drain thoroughly.\nStep 5: Simmering Thick Brown Sugar Syrup - In a small saucepan over medium heat, combine authentic dark muscovado or Taiwanese black sugar with 1/2 cup water. Bring to a simmer, stirring until sugar dissolves. Add drained boba pearls and simmer over medium-low heat for 6 to 8 minutes, stirring frequently, until syrup reduces to a thick, glossy, molasses-like consistency that clings to the pearls.\nStep 6: Creating Tiger Stripe Glass Marbling - Spoon 3 tablespoons of warm boba pearls and thick brown sugar syrup into the bottom of chilled tall glasses. Hold each glass horizontally and rotate it slowly, tilting to let the viscous black sugar syrup trail down the interior glass walls, creating striking tiger stripe marbling.\nStep 7: Ice Loading & Chilled Milk Pour - Pack glasses with crushed or cubed ice. Pour chilled fresh whole milk (or rich oat milk) gently over the ice until glass is nearly full, followed by a splash of concentrated brewed black Assam tea.\nStep 8: Service with Oversized Boba Straw - Insert an extra-wide boba straw to the bottom. Serve immediately while boba pearls are warm and chewy at the bottom and milk is ice-cold on top."
