    avg_rating: 4.8,
    rating_count: 31,
    ingredients: "1 cup long-grain white rice\n2 whole Mexican cinnamon sticks (canela)\n4 cups hot water\n1 can (12 oz) evaporated milk\n1/2 can (7 oz) sweetened condensed milk\n1 tbsp pure Mexican vanilla extract\nCrushed ice and ground cinnamon for dusting",
    steps: "Step 1: Rinsing White Rice & Breaking Canela Quills - Place long-grain white rice in a fine-mesh strainer and rinse under cold water for 60 seconds to wash away excess surface powder. In a heatproof bowl, combine rinsed rice with genuine Mexican canela cinnamon quills, broken into 1-inch pieces.\nStep 2: The Hot Water Overnight Infusion - Pour 3 cups of hot water (approx. 180°F / 82°C) over the rice and cinnamon quills. Cover bowl tightly with a plate and let steep at room temperature for at least 4 hours, or preferably overnight (8 to 12 hours); soaking softens the rice grains and allows cinnamon oils to infuse deeply.\nStep 3: High-Speed Blender Pureeing - Transfer the soaked rice, cinnamon quills, and all soaking water directly into a high-speed blender jar. Blend on high speed for 3 full minutes until completely liquefied and milky white with tiny pulverized cinnamon flecks.\nStep 4: Double Cheesecloth Straining - Place a fine-mesh sieve lined with double-layer cheesecloth or nut milk bag over a clean pitcher. Pour blended rice milk through the cloth. Squeeze bag firmly to extract all liquid while leaving fine chalky rice sediment behind; discard sediment for a silky-smooth drink.\nStep 5: Sweetening with Milks & Real Vanilla - Into the strained rice liquid, whisk evaporated milk, sweetened condensed milk, pure Mexican vanilla extract, and granulated sugar until harmoniously dissolved and creamy.\nStep 6: Chilling to Icy Refreshment - Cover pitcher and refrigerate for at least 2 hours until thoroughly chilled. Stir well with a long bar spoon before serving, as natural rice milk separates on standing.\nStep 7: Glass Ice Preparation - Fill tall highball glasses to the brim with large solid ice cubes.\nStep 8: Garnish & Traditional Service - Pour cold horchata over the ice. Dust the frothy surface with freshly ground Mexican canela cinnamon and garnish with a whole cinnamon stick stirrer.",
    cooking_instructions: "Step 1: Rinsing White Rice & Breaking Canela Quills - Place long-grain white rice in a fine-mesh strainer and rinse under cold water for 60 seconds to wash away excess surface powder. In a heatproof bowl, combine rinsed rice with genuine Mexican canela cinnamon quills, broken into 1-inch pieces.\nStep 2: The Hot Water Overnight Infusion - Pour 3 cups of hot water (approx. 180°F / 82°C) over the rice and cinnamon quills. Cover bowl tightly with a plate and let steep at room temperature for at least 4 hours, or preferably overnight (8 to 12 hours); soaking softens the rice grains and allows cinnamon oils to infuse deeply.\nStep 3: High-Speed Blender Pureeing - Transfer the soaked rice, cinnamon quills, and all soaking water directly into a high-speed blender jar. Blend on high speed for 3 full minutes until completely liquefied and milky white with tiny pulverized cinnamon flecks.\nStep 4: Double Cheesecloth Straining - Place a fine-mesh sieve lined with double-layer cheesecloth or nut milk bag over a clean pitcher. Pour blended rice milk through the cloth. Squeeze bag firmly to extract all liquid while leaving fine chalky rice sediment behind; discard sediment for a silky-smooth drink.\nStep 5: Sweetening with Milks & Real Vanilla - Into the strained rice liquid, whisk evaporated milk, sweetened condensed milk, pure Mexican vanilla extract, and granulated sugar until harmoniously dissolved and creamy.\nStep 6: Chilling to Icy Refreshment - Cover pitcher and refrigerate for at least 2 hours until thoroughly chilled. Stir well with a long bar spoon before serving, as natural rice milk separates on standing.\nStep 7: Glass Ice Preparation - Fill tall highball glasses to the brim with large solid ice cubes.\nStep 8: Garnish & Traditional Service - Pour cold horchata over the ice. Dust the frothy surface with freshly ground Mexican canela cinnamon and garnish with a whole cinnamon stick stirrer."
