    ingredients: "1 cup freshly squeezed Meyer lemon juice (approx. 5-6 lemons, strained)\n1.5 packed cups fresh spearmint leaves (washed, stems removed)\n1/2 cup cane sugar syrup (simple syrup made from equal parts sugar and water)\n3 cups ice cubes\n1/2 cup cold sparkling or still water\nFresh lemon wheels & mint sprigs for garnish",
    steps: "Step 1: Fresh Meyer Lemon Juicing - Squeeze fresh lemons until you have 1 cup of juice. Pour through a fine-mesh sieve into a container to remove all seeds and stringy pulp; clean strained juice is essential for smooth slush texture.\nStep 2: Preparing Chilled Simple Syrup - In a small saucepan, bring equal parts granulated cane sugar and filtered water to a simmer for 2 minutes until sugar dissolves. Chill completely in the refrigerator.\nStep 3: Plucking Tender Spearmint Leaves - Pluck fresh spearmint leaves from stems until you have 1.5 packed cups. Wash thoroughly in cold water and spin dry. Discard all stems completely; stems contain bitter tannins that destroy the sweet, refreshing mint profile.\nStep 4: Blender Base Assembly - In a high-speed blender carafe, combine the strained lemon juice, cooled simple syrup, fresh mint leaves, and 1/2 cup cold water.\nStep 5: Packing with Solid Ice Cubes - Add 3 cups of solid ice cubes directly on top of the liquid in the blender.\nStep 6: High-Power Blending to Frosty Slush - Pulse on high speed for 30 to 45 seconds, stopping once to stir. Blend until ice is crushed into an ultra-fine, frosty green snow slush with tiny emerald mint flecks evenly suspended throughout.\nStep 7: Sugar-Rimmed Glass Prep - Moisten the rims of tall highball glasses with a lemon wedge and dip into granulated sugar to create a sparkling frosted rim.\nStep 8: Garnish & Immediate Frosty Service - Pour the icy green Limonana into chilled glasses. Garnish with a fresh lemon wheel on the rim and a sprig of fresh mint. Serve immediately with a wide straw while frosty."
