    avg_rating: 4.8,
    rating_count: 38,
    ingredients: "3 cups 100% pure unsweetened pomegranate or Concord grape juice\n1 cup freshly squeezed orange juice\n1/4 cup fresh lemon juice\n1 Honeycrisp apple (cored and diced)\n1 Valencia orange & 1 lemon (sliced into thin wheels)\n1 cup fresh blackberries and strawberries (halved)\n1 Ceylon cinnamon stick & 2 whole star anise\n1.5 cups sparkling water or citrus club soda (chilled)\nFresh mint leaves and ice for serving",
    steps: "Step 1: Citrus & Orchard Fruit Slicing - Wash Valencia oranges, Meyer lemons, limes, and Honeycrisp apples thoroughly. Slice citrus into uniform thin wheels and dice apple into bite-sized 1/2-inch cubes. Halve fresh strawberries.\nStep 2: Macerating Fruit with Honey & Spices - Place sliced fruit into a large glass pitcher. Add whole Ceylon cinnamon sticks and whole star anise. Drizzle with raw honey. Using a wooden spoon, gently muddle fruit for 30 seconds to release aromatic citrus oils from the rinds without shredding the pulp.\nStep 3: Pouring Dark Fruit Juices - Pour in 100% pure unsweetened pomegranate juice (or dark Concord grape juice), freshly squeezed orange juice, and fresh lemon juice. Stir with a long bar spoon.\nStep 4: The 2-Hour Cold Infusion - Cover pitcher tightly and refrigerate for at least 2 hours (or overnight); cold steeping allows fruit acids, honey, and whole spices to marry into a complex, wine-like depth.\nStep 5: Ice Loading the Pitcher - Just before serving, pack the pitcher with large, clear ice cubes.\nStep 6: Sparkling Effervescence - Slowly pour chilled sparkling water or citrus club soda down the inside wall of the pitcher, stirring once gently to create a dancing effervescence without losing carbonation.\nStep 7: Glass Pouring with Fruit Gems - Fill wide wine glasses with ice. Pour sangria into glasses, ladling marinated fruit pieces into each serving.\nStep 8: Garnish & Table Service - Top each glass with a fresh mint sprig and a cinnamon stick stirrer. Serve cold."
